NEON for JavaScript
*******************

<div class=perex>

The package `@nette/neon` reads and writes NEON in JavaScript and TypeScript, in Node and in the browser. With it you:

- decode a configuration file into JavaScript values and encode values back to NEON
- [edit a file |editing] while every comment and space you did not touch stays where it was
- check files with `neon-lint` in your editor, in CI or after an AI agent

</div>


Installation
============

```shell
npm install @nette/neon
```

It is an ES module with TypeScript types, for Node 22 or newer or any current browser, and it has no dependencies. Only the command `neon-lint` uses the API of Node.


Decoding and Encoding
=====================

The function `decode()` turns NEON into JavaScript values and `encode()` turns them back:

```js
import { decode, encode } from '@nette/neon';

decode('roles: [guest, admin]');           // { roles: ['guest', 'admin'] }
encode({ roles: ['guest', 'admin'] });     // '{roles: [guest, admin]}'
encode({ roles: ['guest', 'admin'] }, true); // 'roles:\n\t- guest\n\t- admin\n\n'
```

The library does not read files itself, so a file is read by Node:

```js
import { readFileSync } from 'node:fs';

const config = decode(readFileSync('config.neon', 'utf8'));
```

Instead of `true`, the second argument of `encode()` can be a function that decides for each array whether to write it as a block. It gets the array and the list of keys on the way to it:

```js
encode(config, (value, path) => !path.length); // blocks for the first level only
```

The values of the [format] become these JavaScript values:

| NEON | JavaScript
|-----------------------------------------
| `null`, `true`, `yes` … | `null`, `boolean`
| integer | `number`; beyond 2^53 a `bigint`
| float | `number`
| string | `string`
| [date |format#Dates] | `NeonDate`
| [entity |format#Entities], chain | `Entity`
| sequence | `Array`
| mapping | a plain object

A `NeonDate` holds the date as it was written, including a missing time zone; `toDate(zone)` turns it into a `Date`. A mapping is a plain object with its own properties only, so a key such as `__proto__` is just a key.

Two things follow from JavaScript itself. A float without a fractional part, such as `1.0`, is the same `number` as `1` and is written back as `1`. And an object lists keys that look like integers first, in ascending order; the [syntax tree |editing] keeps the order of the file.


Errors
======

When the input is not valid NEON, `decode()` and `parse()` throw `NeonError`. Its property `position` holds the line and the column of the problem:

```js
import { decode, NeonError } from '@nette/neon';

try {
	decode('database:\n\thost: localhost\n\thost: db');
} catch (e) {
	if (e instanceof NeonError) {
		e.message;            // "Duplicated key 'host'"
		e.position.line;      // 3
	}
}
```


Editing Files
=============

The function `parse()` gives the syntax tree of a file, and through it you change the file so that the rest of it stays as its author wrote it:

```js
import { readFileSync, writeFileSync } from 'node:fs';
import { parse } from '@nette/neon';

const doc = parse(readFileSync('config.neon', 'utf8'));
doc.setValue(['database', 'host'], 'db.example.com');
writeFileSync('config.neon', doc.print());
```

How to change, add, remove and rename items, and how to find your way around the tree, is described on the page [Editing Files |editing].


Checking Files: neon-lint
=========================

The package comes with the command `neon-lint`. Give it files or directories, and it prints one line for each problem:

```shell
npx neon-lint config/
```

```
config/local.neon:3:2: Duplicated key 'user'
```

The command, its options and its exit codes are the same as in the [PHP library |php#Checking Files: neon-lint]. To run it without installing the package, use `npx --package=@nette/neon neon-lint config/`.

An AI agent that runs it after every change of a `.neon` file learns about a broken file right away. The [guide for AI agents |https://github.com/nette/neon-js/blob/master/docs/skills/agents.md] shows how to set it up as a hook, and it works whatever the language of the project.

More examples, each with its real output, are in the [repository of the library |https://github.com/nette/neon-js/tree/master/examples].

